Volume = {14759},
Pages = {181-190},
Note = {10th International Conference on Rigorous State-Based Methods (ABZ),
   Bergamo, ITALY, JUN 25-28, 2024},
Abstract = {Cloud computing technologies help developers build scalable distributed
   apps. Serverless architecture, or Function as a Service (FaaS), which
   separates app businesses into multiple functions, is one of the
   cloud-native architectures that has gained popularity. Those functions
   can be developed and deployed independently without provisioning
   infrastructure.
   Despite the considerable advantages and increasing popularity of
   cloud-native apps, developers face many challenges when building their
   cloud-native applications. To ensure the robustness and security of
   cloud-native apps and protect crucial resources, the design and
   implementation of functions and associated access control systems play a
   pivotal role.
   In this paper, we have employed formal methods and tools to develop a
   set of patterns to help cloud-native application developers to design
   robust serverless apps. We have used Event-B and its associated toolset,
   Rodin, to construct these formal patterns and demonstrated how these
   patterns can be used in practical case studies.},
